# Singapore International Arbitration Act 1994 (2020 Rev Ed), s 31

Source: International Arbitration Act 1994 (2020 Rev Ed) (Singapore), s 31.
Pinned URL: see `sg_iaa_s_31_source.yaml`. In force; current text.
Singapore's domestic implementation of New York Convention 1958, Art V.

ATTRIBUTION (per RULES-ATTRIBUTION.md):
- Statute (s 31, IAA): reproduced under the Singapore Statutes Online
  Reproduction Notice (Attorney-General's Chambers, Singapore).
- *DKT v DKU* [2025] SGCA 23, ¶ 8 (four-condition framework, below):
  © Government of Singapore. Quoted under fair-dealing for criticism /
  review per the Copyright Act 2021 (Cap 63), s 191.

## Section 31 — verbatim text

Reproduced verbatim from the Singapore Statutes Online consolidation
(IAA 1994, current version):

31.—(1) In any proceedings in which the enforcement of a foreign award
is sought by virtue of this Part, the party against whom the
enforcement is sought may request that the enforcement be refused, and
the enforcement in any of the cases mentioned in subsections (2) and
(4) may be refused but not otherwise.

(2) A court so requested may refuse enforcement of a foreign award if
the person against whom enforcement is sought proves to the
satisfaction of the court that —

(a) a party to the arbitration agreement pursuant to which the award
    was made was, under the law applicable to the party, under some
    incapacity at the time when the agreement was made;

(b) the arbitration agreement is not valid under the law to which the
    parties have subjected it or, in the absence of any indication in
    that respect, under the law of the country where the award was
    made;

(c) the party was not given proper notice of the appointment of the
    arbitrator or of the arbitration proceedings or was otherwise
    unable to present the party's case in the arbitration proceedings;

(d) subject to subsection (3), the award deals with a difference not
    contemplated by, or not falling within the terms of, the
    submission to arbitration or contains a decision on the matter
    beyond the scope of the submission to arbitration;

(e) the composition of the arbitral authority or the arbitral
    procedure was not in accordance with the agreement of the parties
    or, failing such agreement, was not in accordance with the law of
    the country where the arbitration took place; or

(f) the award has not yet become binding on the parties to the
    arbitral award or has been set aside or suspended by a competent
    authority of the country in which, or under the law of which, the
    award was made.

(3) When a foreign award referred to in subsection (2)(d) contains
decisions on matters not submitted to arbitration but those decisions
can be separated from decisions on matters submitted to arbitration,
the award may be enforced to the extent that it contains decisions on
matters so submitted.

(4) In any proceedings in which the enforcement of a foreign award is
sought by virtue of this Part, the court may refuse to enforce the
award if it finds that —

(a) the subject matter of the difference between the parties to the
    award is not capable of settlement by arbitration under the law of
    Singapore; or

(b) enforcement of the award would be contrary to the public policy of
    Singapore.

(5) Where, in any proceedings in which the enforcement of a foreign
award is sought by virtue of this Part, the court is satisfied that an
application for the setting aside or for the suspension of the award
has been made to a competent authority of the country in which, or
under the law of which, the award was made, the court may —

(a) if the court considers it proper to do so, adjourn the proceedings
    or (as the case may be) so much of the proceedings as relates to
    the award; and

(b) on the application of the party seeking to enforce the award,
    order the other party to give suitable security.

The court's disposition is one of:
  - *ApplicationDismissedEntirely* — no ground made out
  - *ApplicationAllowedInPart* — at least one ground partially made out;
    the relevant order is excised per-paragraph but the award stands
  - *AwardSetAside* — at least one ground fully made out; no enforcement

## Encoding

```catala
declaration enumeration IAAGroundId:
  -- S31_2_a_Incapacity
  -- S31_2_b_InvalidAgreement
  -- S31_2_c_NaturalJustice
  -- S31_2_d_OutsideScope
  -- S31_2_e_TribunalComposition
  -- S31_2_f_NotBindingOrSetAside
  -- S31_4_a_NotArbitrable
  -- S31_4_b_PublicPolicy

declaration enumeration GroundOutcome:
  -- Dismissed
  -- AllowedInPart
  -- AllowedInFull

declaration enumeration ApplicationDisposition:
  -- ApplicationDismissedEntirely
  -- ApplicationAllowedInPart
  -- AwardSetAside

declaration structure GroundFinding:
  data ground content IAAGroundId
  data court_outcome content GroundOutcome
  ## s 31(3) severability proviso. Applies only to ground (d)
  ## (OutsideScope). When true and the ground is AllowedInFull, the
  ## disposition becomes AllowedInPart at the application level
  ## because the in-scope decisions can be enforced separately.
  data is_severable content boolean

declaration structure RefusalDisposition:
  data n_grounds_pleaded content integer
  data n_grounds_dismissed content integer
  data n_grounds_partial content integer
  data n_grounds_full content integer
  data application_disposition content ApplicationDisposition
  data award_enforced content boolean

declaration scope IAA_S31_Refusal:
  input grounds content list of GroundFinding
  output disposition content RefusalDisposition

scope IAA_S31_Refusal:
  definition disposition equals
    let dismissed equals
      number of (list of g among grounds
                 such that g.court_outcome = Dismissed)
    in
    let partial equals
      number of (list of g among grounds
                 such that g.court_outcome = AllowedInPart)
    in
    let full equals
      number of (list of g among grounds
                 such that g.court_outcome = AllowedInFull)
    in
    ## A "fully allowed" ground is dispositive UNLESS it is the
    ## OutsideScope ground (d) AND severable per s 31(3) — then the
    ## dispositive shape is partial enforcement. Other fully-allowed
    ## grounds (incapacity, invalid agreement, natural justice,
    ## tribunal composition, not-binding, not-arbitrable, public
    ## policy) admit no severability under s 31(3).
    let full_with_severability_relief equals
      number of (list of g among grounds
                 such that g.court_outcome = AllowedInFull
                       and g.ground = S31_2_d_OutsideScope
                       and g.is_severable)
    in
    let full_dispositive equals full - full_with_severability_relief in
    let app equals
      if full_dispositive > 0 then AwardSetAside
      else (if partial > 0 or full_with_severability_relief > 0 then
              ApplicationAllowedInPart
            else ApplicationDismissedEntirely)
    in
    RefusalDisposition {
      -- n_grounds_pleaded: number of grounds
      -- n_grounds_dismissed: dismissed
      -- n_grounds_partial: partial
      -- n_grounds_full: full
      -- application_disposition: app
      -- award_enforced: full_dispositive = 0
    }
```

## *DKT v DKU* [2025] SGCA 23, [2025] 1 SLR 806 — four-condition framework

Sub-rule under s 31(2)(c) (natural-justice / inability to present case)
and s 31(4)(b) (public policy). The Singapore Court of Appeal in
*DKT v DKU* set out the framework for an "infra petita" challenge —
where the tribunal allegedly failed to consider an essential point
that was placed before it.

Reproduced verbatim from paragraph 8 of the judgment ([2025] SGCA 23):

"a successful infra petita challenge can only be mounted if all of the
following four conditions are satisfied"

  (a) "the point must have been properly brought before the tribunal
      for its determination";

  (b) "the point must have been essential to the resolution of the
      dispute";

  (c) "the tribunal must have completely failed to consider the
      point"; and

  (d) "even if the tribunal failed to consider an essential point
      placed before it, there must have been real or actual prejudice".

Condition (c) is reinforced elsewhere in the judgment: the court must
be able to draw a "clear and virtually inescapable inference" that the
tribunal failed to consider the point at all (not merely that it
weighed the point and reached an answer the applicant disagrees with).

The conditions are conjunctive — failure of any one defeats the
challenge.

```catala
declaration structure DKTConditions:
  data point_properly_before_tribunal content boolean
  data point_essential_to_dispute content boolean
  data tribunal_completely_failed_to_consider content boolean
  data prejudice_demonstrated content boolean

declaration scope DKTvDKUChallenge:
  input conditions content DKTConditions
  output challenge_succeeds content boolean

scope DKTvDKUChallenge:
  definition challenge_succeeds equals
    conditions.point_properly_before_tribunal
    and conditions.point_essential_to_dispute
    and conditions.tribunal_completely_failed_to_consider
    and conditions.prejudice_demonstrated
```

## s 31(5) — adjournment and security

Reproduced verbatim from s 31(5) IAA: "Where, in any proceedings in
which the enforcement of a foreign award is sought by virtue of this
Part, the court is satisfied that an application for the setting
aside or for the suspension of the award has been made to a competent
authority of the country in which, or under the law of which, the
award was made, the court may — (a) if the court considers it proper
to do so, adjourn the proceedings or (as the case may be) so much of
the proceedings as relates to the award; and (b) on the application
of the party seeking to enforce the award, order the other party to
give suitable security."

This is an orthogonal limb: it does not change the disposition on the
merits but reflects a discretionary case-management power the court
has *while* a parallel set-aside application is pending at the seat.

```catala
declaration structure S31_5_Inputs:
  data setting_aside_pending_at_seat content boolean
  data adjournment_ordered content boolean
  data security_ordered content boolean

declaration structure S31_5_Output:
  data proceedings_adjourned content boolean
  data security_required content boolean
  data adjournment_engaged_lawfully content boolean

declaration scope IAA_S31_5_Adjournment:
  input inputs content S31_5_Inputs
  output adjournment content S31_5_Output

scope IAA_S31_5_Adjournment:
  definition adjournment equals
    ## The court's adjournment / security order is lawful only where
    ## a parallel set-aside application is pending at the seat.
    ## (a) and (b) are independent: the court can adjourn without
    ## ordering security, or order security without adjourning.
    let lawful equals inputs.setting_aside_pending_at_seat in
    S31_5_Output {
      -- proceedings_adjourned: inputs.adjournment_ordered and lawful
      -- security_required: inputs.security_ordered and lawful
      -- adjournment_engaged_lawfully: lawful
    }
```

## DKT v DKU + s 31(2)(c) composition

Where an applicant pleads s 31(2)(c) (natural justice / inability to
present case) on an infra-petita basis — alleging the tribunal
completely failed to consider an essential point — DKT v DKU's
four-condition framework determines whether the ground is made out.

The composition scope below takes both the DKT conditions and the
court's stated outcome on the s 31(2)(c) ground, computes the DKT
result, and reports a consistency check: a `dkt_analysis_consistent`
flag is set true iff the court's stated outcome matches what the DKT
framework would yield. A surfaced inconsistency does not override the
court's disposition — it surfaces a question for review (the kind of
machine-verifiable discrepancy this protocol exists to flag).

```catala
declaration structure DKTComposite:
  data conditions content DKTConditions
  data court_outcome_on_31_2_c content GroundOutcome
  data dkt_challenge_succeeds content boolean
  data dkt_analysis_consistent content boolean

declaration scope IAA_S31_2_c_InfraPetita:
  input conditions content DKTConditions
  input court_outcome_on_31_2_c content GroundOutcome
  output composite content DKTComposite

scope IAA_S31_2_c_InfraPetita:
  definition composite equals
    let challenge_succeeds equals
      conditions.point_properly_before_tribunal
      and conditions.point_essential_to_dispute
      and conditions.tribunal_completely_failed_to_consider
      and conditions.prejudice_demonstrated
    in
    let consistent equals
      ## DKT challenge succeeds <=> court outcome AllowedInFull.
      ## A partial outcome on infra-petita grounds is structurally
      ## unusual — the four conditions are conjunctive — so we treat
      ## any non-{Dismissed, AllowedInFull} outcome as inconsistent.
      if challenge_succeeds then
        court_outcome_on_31_2_c = AllowedInFull
      else
        court_outcome_on_31_2_c = Dismissed
    in
    DKTComposite {
      -- conditions: conditions
      -- court_outcome_on_31_2_c: court_outcome_on_31_2_c
      -- dkt_challenge_succeeds: challenge_succeeds
      -- dkt_analysis_consistent: consistent
    }
```

## Used in

trace-06 — SIC/OA 9/2025 *GNC Holdings v ONI Global* [2025] SGHC(I) 25.

## Tests

```catala
#[test] declaration scope TestIAA_S31_TraceShape:
  composite scope IAA_S31_Refusal
  output disposition content RefusalDisposition

scope TestIAA_S31_TraceShape:
  definition disposition equals composite.disposition
  ## Four grounds pleaded, three dismissed, one allowed in part — the
  ## same shape as SIC/OA 9/2025 (trace-06).
  definition composite.grounds equals [
    GroundFinding { -- ground: S31_4_b_PublicPolicy        -- court_outcome: Dismissed     -- is_severable: false };
    GroundFinding { -- ground: S31_2_c_NaturalJustice      -- court_outcome: Dismissed     -- is_severable: false };
    GroundFinding { -- ground: S31_2_d_OutsideScope        -- court_outcome: Dismissed     -- is_severable: false };
    GroundFinding { -- ground: S31_2_d_OutsideScope        -- court_outcome: AllowedInPart -- is_severable: true  }
  ]
  assertion disposition.application_disposition = ApplicationAllowedInPart
  assertion disposition.award_enforced = true
  assertion disposition.n_grounds_dismissed = 3
  assertion disposition.n_grounds_partial = 1

#[test] declaration scope TestDKTvDKU_AllConditionsMet:
  composite scope DKTvDKUChallenge
  output challenge_succeeds content boolean

scope TestDKTvDKU_AllConditionsMet:
  definition challenge_succeeds equals composite.challenge_succeeds
  definition composite.conditions equals
    DKTConditions {
      -- point_properly_before_tribunal: true
      -- point_essential_to_dispute: true
      -- tribunal_completely_failed_to_consider: true
      -- prejudice_demonstrated: true
    }
  assertion challenge_succeeds = true

#[test] declaration scope TestDKTvDKU_OneConditionFails:
  composite scope DKTvDKUChallenge
  output challenge_succeeds content boolean

scope TestDKTvDKU_OneConditionFails:
  definition challenge_succeeds equals composite.challenge_succeeds
  ## In trace-06, the second condition (point essential to the dispute)
  ## was not made out.
  definition composite.conditions equals
    DKTConditions {
      -- point_properly_before_tribunal: true
      -- point_essential_to_dispute: false
      -- tribunal_completely_failed_to_consider: false
      -- prejudice_demonstrated: false
    }
  assertion challenge_succeeds = false

#[test] declaration scope TestIAA_S31_SeverabilityRelief:
  composite scope IAA_S31_Refusal
  output disposition content RefusalDisposition

scope TestIAA_S31_SeverabilityRelief:
  definition disposition equals composite.disposition
  ## A fully-allowed (d) ground that is severable per s 31(3) yields
  ## ApplicationAllowedInPart with the award still enforced (the
  ## in-scope decisions). Compare TestIAA_S31_FullSetAside_NoSeverability
  ## below where the same fact-pattern without severability yields
  ## AwardSetAside.
  definition composite.grounds equals [
    GroundFinding { -- ground: S31_2_d_OutsideScope -- court_outcome: AllowedInFull -- is_severable: true }
  ]
  assertion disposition.application_disposition = ApplicationAllowedInPart
  assertion disposition.award_enforced = true

#[test] declaration scope TestIAA_S31_FullSetAside_NoSeverability:
  composite scope IAA_S31_Refusal
  output disposition content RefusalDisposition

scope TestIAA_S31_FullSetAside_NoSeverability:
  definition disposition equals composite.disposition
  ## Same OutsideScope ground but NOT severable: no s 31(3) relief,
  ## the award is set aside.
  definition composite.grounds equals [
    GroundFinding { -- ground: S31_2_d_OutsideScope -- court_outcome: AllowedInFull -- is_severable: false }
  ]
  assertion disposition.application_disposition = AwardSetAside
  assertion disposition.award_enforced = false

#[test] declaration scope TestIAA_S31_NaturalJusticeNotSeverable:
  composite scope IAA_S31_Refusal
  output disposition content RefusalDisposition

scope TestIAA_S31_NaturalJusticeNotSeverable:
  definition disposition equals composite.disposition
  ## Even with `is_severable: true`, a fully-allowed natural-justice
  ## ground (s 31(2)(c)) does not get severability relief — the
  ## proviso in s 31(3) applies only to ground (d). Award is set aside.
  definition composite.grounds equals [
    GroundFinding { -- ground: S31_2_c_NaturalJustice -- court_outcome: AllowedInFull -- is_severable: true }
  ]
  assertion disposition.application_disposition = AwardSetAside
  assertion disposition.award_enforced = false
```
